<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Bell Pepper</h1></b><br>

    Bell peppers, also known as sweet peppers, can be successfully grown in the UK, though they require 
    <strong>warmer conditions</strong> than some other crops. They grow best in <strong>southern regions</strong>, 
    where summers are warmer, but they can also be cultivated in <strong>greenhouses</strong> or <strong>polytunnels</strong> 
    in <strong>northern England and Scotland</strong>. Due to their sensitivity to cold, outdoor cultivation is only recommended 
    in sheltered, sunny locations during the warmest months.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Best Growing Conditions</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Climate:</strong> Bell peppers need warm temperatures (above 15°C) to thrive.<br>
    - <strong>Soil:</strong> Well-drained, nutrient-rich soil with a pH between 6.0 and 6.8.<br>
    - <strong>Sunlight:</strong> Full sun is essential for good fruit development.<br>
    - <strong>Watering:</strong> Keep the soil consistently moist but not waterlogged.<br>
    - <strong>Spacing:</strong> Space plants around 40–50cm apart to allow good airflow.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Common Bell Pepper Diseases in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Bacterial Spot:</strong>  
      - <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Small, dark, water-soaked spots on leaves and fruit, often with a yellow halo.<br>
      - <strong>Prevention & Treatment:</strong>  
        - Avoid overhead watering to reduce moisture on leaves.<br>
        - Use disease-free seeds and resistant varieties when possible.<br>
        - Remove infected leaves and apply copper-based fungicides if needed.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Tips for Growing Bell Peppers in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Start Indoors:</strong> Since peppers need warm temperatures, start seeds indoors 6–8 weeks before the last frost.<br>
    - <strong>Harden Off Before Planting:</strong> Gradually expose young plants to outdoor conditions before transplanting.<br>
    - <strong>Use Mulch:</strong> Mulching helps retain moisture and keeps soil temperatures stable.<br>
    - <strong>Support Plants:</strong> Staking or caging may be needed to prevent stems from breaking under the weight of the fruit.<br>
    - <strong>Harvest Regularly:</strong> Picking ripe peppers encourages more fruit production.<br><br>

    With proper care and a warm growing environment, bell peppers can thrive in the UK, especially when grown in greenhouses or polytunnels.
    </i></p>
